<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Places I Can Go – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Places I Can Go</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm at School.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of simple spoken instructions. Interactive: Identify the main points in a conversation.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can demonstrate comprehension and perform tasks based on oral instructions related to identifying location (e.g., "Point to the house."). Can identify the main points of a conversation about specific places or locations (e.g., recognizing “market” in a short chant or sentence). Can recognize words and phrases about places from one peer to another (e.g., "I'm at school." "Where is she?" "She's at school.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f7f8;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, plays a short "Listen and Point" game. The teacher says familiar classroom objects or areas (e.g., "Point to the board!", "Point to the door!", "Point to your chair!") and students physically point. This activates listening skills and prepares for new locations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces new vocabulary for places using large, clear flashcards or realia (if possible). Words introduced: <i>school, market, park, home, library, store, cafeteria, playground</i>. For each word, the teacher holds up the flashcard, says the word clearly (e.g., "School. This is a school."), and models the pronunciation, explicitly focusing on /sk/ in "school" and /m/ in "market". Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher also introduces "I'm at (place)" by pointing to him/herself and the flashcard (e.g., "I'm at school.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a school) "Is this a market?" (Expected answer: "No, it's a school.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a park) "Where am I?" (Expected answer: "You're at the park.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the classroom) "Are we at school?" (Expected answer: "Yes, we're at school.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Places I Can Go")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher plays a short, simple audio recording (or reads aloud) of a dialogue between two children talking about where they are. The dialogue uses the target vocabulary: <i>school, market, park, home</i>, and the grammar "I'm at..." and "Are you at...?"
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">Example Dialogue:</p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">Child 1: "Hi, Ana! Where are you?"</p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">Child 2: "Hi, Tom! I'm at school."</p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">Child 1: "Oh, I'm at the market."</p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">Child 2: "Are you at the park?"</p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">Child 1: "No, I'm at home."</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Can identify the main points of a conversation about specific places or locations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (listening):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher asks general questions after the first listening. "Who is talking?" "Are they happy?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific Information:</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students are given a worksheet with pictures of a school, market, park, and home. As they listen, they point to the picture of the place mentioned. The teacher then asks: "Is Ana at the park?" (True/False or Yes/No). "Where is Tom?" (Students point to the correct picture).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Contractions ("I'm"), prepositions of place ("at school"), present simple ("I'm at school."). Vocabulary: <i>school, market, park, home, library, store, cafeteria, playground</i>. Pronunciation: /sk/ in "school", /m/ in "market", phonemic awareness /ɪn/ for "in". Pragmatic: Describing locations ("I'm at school.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                        <ol>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Listen and Repeat/Point):</b> The teacher says a word or a short phrase (e.g., "school", "at the market", "I'm at home"). Students listen and repeat chorally and individually, focusing on clear pronunciation of /sk/ and /m/. Then, the teacher says a word, and students point to the corresponding flashcard or picture on the board. The teacher models saying "Thank you!" when students respond correctly.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Listen and Complete the Sentence):</b> The teacher says a sentence with a missing place, and students listen and complete it orally or by choosing from a visual list. E.g., Teacher: "I'm at the..." (holds up picture of a park). Students: "Park!" Teacher: "Please say the whole sentence." Students: "I'm at the park." Alternatively, the teacher says a sentence (e.g., "I'm at the library.") and students listen, then point to the correct picture on their desks or a worksheet.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Listen and Follow):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has a set of flashcards with the target places. Student A says, "Point to the school!" or "I'm at the market." Student B listens and points to the correct flashcard. They take turns, practicing listening to each other's simple instructions and statements about location. The teacher circulates, providing feedback on pronunciation and comprehension.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Places I Can Go")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Listen and Draw the Path" using a simple town map.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connects directly to Theme: "I'm at School." and Scenario: "Places I Can Go":</b> Students are given a simple map of a small town with several of the learned places clearly labeled and depicted (school, market, park, home, library, store). The teacher gives oral instructions for moving between places.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion:</b> The teacher models the activity first. "Start at home. Go to the market. Now, go to the school." Students listen carefully and draw a line on their map following the instructions. The teacher gives 3-4 different sets of instructions. Students show their paths to a partner to compare (peer check on listening comprehension). The teacher also gives instructions with "I'm at..." statements: "I'm at the park. Now I go to the library." This activity reinforces listening for specific place names and understanding the concept of "at" a place.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and "Listen and Mark" Worksheet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a worksheet with 4-5 pictures of the target places (school, market, park, home, library). The teacher reads a series of simple instructions or statements.
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">Examples:</p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">1. "Circle the park."</p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">2. "Put an 'X' on the school."</p>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">3. "I'm at the market. Draw a star on the market."</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly identifies spoken words for places (e.g., "school", "market").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurately follows simple oral instructions involving location.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates comprehension of "I'm at (place)."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand words about places like 'school' and 'market'." (Circle: Yes / A Little / No)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can follow directions like 'Point to the library'." (Circle: Yes / A Little / No)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new places did you hear about today?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to understand the teacher's instructions? Why?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What activities worked best for engaging students and facilitating listening comprehension for 3rd graders at A1.1? Were the CCQs effective? What adjustments are needed for students who struggled with identifying specific words or following multi-step instructions? Was the pronunciation modeling clear enough for /sk/ and /m/? How can I scaffold more for quieter students?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a short text or story that uses the same vocabulary of places to further develop their understanding and connection to the theme "I'm at School." and scenario "Places I Can Go."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>